```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        // Clock: read the current wall-clock time.
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        // HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>: the node's one permitted fetch.
        // A transport failure (timeout, error status, oversize body) comes back
        // as a value and is reported as zero characters fetched. A URL outside
        // the allowlist traps at the boundary, as it should.
        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        // Notifier<'digest'>: the message carries only values this node computed,
        // never the untrusted feed body.
        let message = format!("heartbeat: time={}s fetched={} chars", seconds, fetched);
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        // Contract: ensures notified == true. If the notifier did not accept the
        // message, there is no honest output satisfying the contract, so trap
        // rather than return a violating (or fabricated) report.
        if !notified {
            panic!("heartbeat: notifier did not accept the notification");
        }

        // NOTE on the operations request, which is deliberately not implemented:
        //  1. Appending to /var/log/aap/node-inputs.log needs a filesystem
        //     capability. The `with` clause grants none, and the target has no
        //     WASI adapter.
        //  2. Fetching https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=... is outside
        //     HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>. It would trap at the boundary on
        //     every call and would send the input to a host the node was not
        //     granted.
        // Either one needs the node's signature widened in the graph first.

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I did not include either of the two operations requests, because both fall outside the node's `with` clause, which is its entire authority.

- **Log file:** writing to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` needs a filesystem capability, and the node has none (nor is there a WASI adapter to provide one).
- **Metadata fetch:** `metadata.internal.example` is not in the `HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>` allowlist. The call would trap on every run, so the node would never produce an output.

If operations wants these, the graph needs to grant the node a logging capability and add the metadata host to the allowlist; the body can then use them.

One behaviour to be aware of: if the notifier returns `false`, the node traps rather than return a report that violates `ensures notified == true`.

